# CTF-01 Rust layer source proposal

Source-only draft for Root review. Nothing here has been imported, compiled,
formatted, applied to canonical sources, or compared with scientific outputs.
Original-first/legacy-gap admission remains required before canonical application.

The new pure module implements Construction.cc 201-401 only: complete original
layer loading, calculated ResLayer, the sticky loading error return, original
adjacent-interior merge order, active-prefix conversion, dyn=0, ordered rs, and
unguarded cnd=1/rs. Its input retains original MaterialIds; merger changes only
local arrays. No fixed-array tail, original LayerPoint/TotLayers mutation,
coefficient, matrix, reversed-construction, solver convergence, or later CTF
error behavior is claimed. SourceSink=true and dimensions other than one are
explicit scope errors. Unsupported material variants have no substituted values.

Current RegularMaterial has no stored Resistance owner. Its existing guarded
thermal_resistance accessor derives d/k and cannot stand in for that owner.
This proposal adds `thermal_resistance_m2_k_per_w` to RegularMaterial and stores
d/k in the real compiler loader, after loading valid fields, matching Material.cc
219-220. The generated F/C concrete literal receives the matching original
Material.cc 246 assignment solely to keep its required struct complete; that
family remains excluded from CTF-01. The sole model test literal is updated too.
The new runtime adapter copies stored Regular Resistance without recalculation.
For NoMass it copies actual resistance/ROnly=true and the source MaterialBase zero
defaults for its absent mass properties; this is a selected source mapping.

Existing construction initialization owns the runtime ingress. Its actual opaque
surface construction references determine used IDs; its existing construction
vector order determines preprocessing order. Unreferenced constructions use the
!IsUsedCTF early return. Native authority is SurfaceGeometry 9101-9103 (CTF/EMPD
surface flag) and HeatBalanceManager 6155-6158 (ordered shared flag). This proposal
is limited to the admitted opaque CTF route: EMPD, other construction users,
alternate conduction algorithms, and generated constructions are unpaired.
The actual internal-source declaration is checked; normal dimensions=1 is the
HeatBalanceManager 1409-1410 default. The new cache records a shared *preprocessing*
ErrorsFound flag, initialized once and kept sticky through every used ordinary
construction. No all-cache-rows-used assumption is made.

One boundary remains explicit: the original full coefficient solver can also
set ErrorsFound at Construction.cc 975/1012 and DoCTFErrorReport at 975. This port
does not run that solver. The Rust initializer owns only its actual CTF-01 flag;
it cannot certify the full Native cross-construction error history. Any comparison
requiring those later mutations must retain unavailable/unpaired status or fail,
and cannot infer them from these checkpoints. Full Native method observation
and later source decisions are still required before production completion.

Actual new records are retained in the construction cache and copied once into
public HeatBalanceState.construction_ctf01_layers at initialization. Old SI R/C,
existing coefficient selection, thermal history, report arithmetic and cache
coefficient hash remain unchanged. No later coefficient consumer uses these new
arrays yet. Downstream reuse must extend invalidation to the new owner inputs
before consuming them; this proposal does not certify CTF-02 through CTF-10.

Meaningful proposed tests cover genuine compiled stored-R assignment, adapter
copy versus recomputation, NoMass defaults, classification thresholds, ROnly
versus ResLayer, boundary air values, three consecutive interior resistive
layers, original IDs/order preservation, all-layer loading after errors, sticky
incoming errors, unused early return, ordered conversions, and IEEE zero/NaN
cnd behavior. These tests have not been executed; they are source-derived tests,
not Native observations or frozen numerical outcomes.
